The first decisive fact is already clear from the paper: small “above-average” parameter \(k\) is explicitly FPT, with \(O(m)+2^{O(k^2)}\) time and an \(O(k^2)\)-variable kernel. So the prior triage is only viable if the generated \(k\) grows with instance size; a small planted surplus would be disqualified as an easy regime. I’m now reading the algebraic reduction and the paper’s practical-discussion section to pin down the exact boundary and certificate route.
