   396	\section{MAX-$r$-SAT}\label{sec3}
   397	
   398	In this section we describe a polynomial-time data reduction that reduces an instance of $\MST$ into an equivalent algebraically represented problem. The equivalent algebraically represented problems is `normalized' in a sense, which enables us to obtain a bound on the size of a given instance. Some results from probability theory and Harmonic analysis in boolean functions play a central role in proving such a bound. As a result, we prove that $\MST$ is fixed-parameter tractable and in particular we present a quadratic kernel using the notion of bikernelization introduced in the previous section.
   399	
   400	
   401	
   402	\subsection{An Algebraic Representation}\label{algerep}
   403	
   404	Let $F$ be an $r$-CNF formula with clauses $C_1,\ldots ,C_m$
   405	in the variables $x_1, x_2, \ldots ,x_n$.
   406	
   407	For $F$, consider
   408	\[
   409	X=\sum_{C\in F}[1-\prod_{x_i\in \var(C)}(1+\epsilon_ix_i)],
   410	\]
   411	where $\epsilon_i\in \{-1,1\}$ and $\epsilon_i=-1$ if and only if $x_i$
   412	is in~$C$.
   413	
   414	\begin{lemma}\label{lem0}
   415	  For a truth assignment $\tau$, we have
   416	  $X=2^r(\sat(\tau,F)-(1-2^{-r})m)$.
   417	\end{lemma}
   418	\begin{proof}
   419	  Observe that $\prod_{x_i\in \var(C)}(1+\epsilon_ix_i)$ equals $2^r$ if
   420	  $C$ is falsified and 0, otherwise. Thus, $X=m-2^r(m-\sat(\tau,F))$
   421	  implying the claimed formula.
   422	\end{proof}
   423	
   424	After algebraic simplification $X=X(x_1, x_2, \ldots ,x_n)$
   425	can be written as
   426	$\label{Xeq}X=\sum_{I\in {\cal S}}X_I,$ where $X_I=c_I\prod_{i\in
   427	I}x_i$, each $c_I$ is a nonzero integer and $\cal S$ is a family of
   428	nonempty subsets of $\{1,\ldots ,n\}$ each with at most $r$
   429	elements.
   430	
   431	The question we address is that of deciding whether or not there are
   432	values $x_i \in \{-1,1\}$ so that $X=X(x_1,x_2, \ldots ,x_n) \geq
   433	k$. The idea is to use a probabilistic argument and show that if the
   434	above polynomial has many nonzero coefficients, that is, if $|{\cal S}|$
   435	is large, this is necessarily the case, whereas if it is small, the
   436	question can be solved by checking all possibilities of the relevant
   437	variables.
   438	
   439	\subsection{The Properties of $X$}
   440	
   441	In what follows, we assume that each variable $x_i$ takes its values
   442	uniformly at random and independently in $\{-1,1\}$ and thus $X$ is a random
   443	variable. Our approach is similar to the one
   444	in~\cite{AlonGutinKrivelevich04}. For completeness, we reproduce part of
   445	the argument (modifying it a bit and slightly improving the constant for
   446	the case considered here).  We need the following simple lemma.
   447	
   448	\begin{lemma}[\normalfont see, e.g., \cite{AlonGutinKrivelevich04}, Lemma 3.1]
   449	  \label{l91}
   450	  For every real random variable $X$ with finite and positive fourth
   451	  moment,
   452	  \[
   453	  \Exp(|X|) \geq \frac{\Exp(X^2)^{3/2}}{\Exp(X^4)^{1/2}}.
   454	  \]
   455	\end{lemma}
   456	The above lemma implies the following (see
   457	\cite{AlonGutinKrivelevich04}, Lemma 3.2, part (ii) for a similar
   458	result).
   459	\begin{corollary}
   460	\label{c93}
   461	Let $X$ be a real random variable and suppose that its first, second and
   462	fourth moments satisfy $\Exp(X)=0$, $\Exp(X^2)=\sigma^2>0$ and $\Exp(X^4)
   463	\leq b \sigma^4$, where $b$ is a positive constant. Then
   464	\[
   465	\Prob(X \geq \frac{\sigma}{2 \sqrt b})>0.
   466	\]
   467	\end{corollary}
   468	\begin{proof}
   469	  By Lemma~\ref{l91}, $\Exp(|X|) \geq \frac{\sigma}{\sqrt b}$. Since
   470	  $\Exp(X)=0$ it follows that
   471	  \begin{equation}
   472	    \label{e91}
   473	    \Prob(X>0)\cdot \Exp(X| X >0) \geq
   474	    \frac{\sigma}{2 \sqrt b}.
   475	\end{equation}
   476	Therefore, $X$ must be at least $\sigma/(2 \sqrt b)$ with positive
   477	probability.
   478	\end{proof}
   479	
   480	\noindent We also use the hypercontractive inequality, see ~\cite{ODonnell08}. The following lemma states a special case of it first proved by Bonami \cite{Bo70}.
   481	
   482	\begin{lemma}[Hypercontractive Inequality]  \label{l92}
   483	  Let $f=f(x_1,\ldots,x_n)$ be a multilinear polynomial of degree (at most) $r$ in $n$
   484	  variables $x_1,\ldots,x_n$ with domain $\{-1,1\}$.  Define a random
   485	  variable $X$ by choosing a vector $(\ep_1,\ldots,\ep_n)\in \{-1,1\}^n$
   486	  uniformly at random and setting $X=f(\ep_1,\ldots,\ep_n)$. Then,
   487	  $\Exp(X^4 ) \leq 9^r (\Exp(X^2))^{2}$.
   488	
   489	\end{lemma}
   490	
   491	\noindent Returning to the \hbox{random variable $X\!=\!X(x_1,x_2,
   492	  \ldots ,x_n)$} defined in the previous subsection, we prove the
   493	following.
   494	
   495	
   496	
   497	\begin{lemma}
   498	  \label{l94}
   499	  Let $X= \sum_{I\in {\cal S}}X_I,$ where $X_I=c_I\prod_{i\in I}x_i$ is
   500	  as in the previous subsection, and assume it is not identically
   501	  zero. Then $\Exp(X)=0$, $\Exp(X^2)=\sum_{I\in {\cal S}}c^2_I \geq
   502	  |{\cal S}| >0$ and $\Exp(X^4)\le 9^r\Exp(X^2)^2$.
   503	\end{lemma}
   504	\begin{proof}
   505	  Since the $x_i$'s are mutually independent, $\Exp(X)=0$. Note that for $I,
   506	  J\in {\cal S}$, $I\neq J$, we have
   507	  $\Exp(X_IX_J)=c_Ic_J\Exp(\prod_{i\in I\mathrel{\Delta} J}x_i)=0,$ where
   508	  $I\mathrel{\Delta} J$ is the symmetric difference of $I$ and~$J$. Thus,
   509	  $\Exp(X^2)=\sum_{I\in {\cal S}}c^2_I$. By Lemma~\ref{l92},
   510	  $\Exp(X^4)\le 9^r\Exp(X^2)^2$.
   511	\end{proof}
   512	
   513	\subsection{The Main Result for General  $r$}
   514	\begin{theorem}
   515	  \label{t95}
   516	  The problem $\MST$ is fixed-parameter tractable and can be solved in
   517	  time $O(m) + 2^{O(k^2)}$. Moreover, there exist
   518	(i) a polynomial-size bikernel from {\MST} to {\MLT},
   519	  and (ii) a polynomial-size kernel of {\MST}. In fact, there are
   520	such a bikernel and a kernel of size $O(k^2)$.
   521	\end{theorem}
   522	\vspace{-5pt}
   523	\begin{proof}
   524	  By Lemma~\ref{lem0} our problem is equivalent to that of deciding
   525	  whether or not there is a truth assignment to the variables $x_1, x_2,
   526	  \ldots, x_n$, so that
   527	\begin{equation}
   528	\label{e1}
   529	X(x_1, \ldots ,x_n) \geq k.
   530	\end{equation}
   531	Note that in particular this implies that if
   532	  $X$ is the zero polynomial, then any truth assignment satisfies
   533	  exactly a $(1-2^{-r})$ fraction of the original clauses.  By
   534	  Corollary~\ref{c93} and Lemma~\ref{l94}, $\Prob(X \geq
   535	  \frac{\sqrt{\Exp(X^2)}}{2\sqrt{b}})>0,$ where $b=9^r$ and
   536	  $\Exp(X^2)=\sum_{I\in {\cal S}}c^2_I \geq |{\cal S}|$; the last
   537	  inequality follows from the fact that each $|c_I|$ is a positive
   538	  integer. Therefore $\Prob(X \geq \frac{\sqrt{ |{\cal S}|}}{2\cdot
   539	    3^r})>0$. Now, if $k\le \frac{\sqrt{|{\cal S}|}}{2\cdot 3^r}$ then
   540	there are $x_i \in \{-1,1\}$ such that
   541	  (\ref{e1}) holds,
   542	and there is an assignment for which the answer to $\MST$
   543	  is {\sc Yes}. Otherwise, $|{\cal S}| = O(k^2)$, and in fact even
   544	$\sum_{I\in {\cal S}}|c_I| \leq \sum_{I\in {\cal S}}c^2_I =O(k^2)$,
   545	that is, the total number of terms of the simplified polynomial,
   546	even when counted with multiplicities, is at most $O(k^2)$.
   547	
   548	  For any fixed $r$, the representation of a problem instance of $m$
   549	  clauses as a polynomial, and the simplification of this polynomial,
   550	  can be performed in time $O(m)$. If the number of nonzero terms of
   551	  this polynomial is larger than $4 \cdot 3^{2r} k^2$, then the answer
   552	  to the problem is {\sc Yes}. Otherwise, the polynomial has at most
   553	  $O(k^2)$ terms and depends on at most $O(k^2)$ variables, and its
   554	  maximum can be found in time $2^{O(k^2)}$.
   555	
   556	This completes the proof of the first part of the theorem.
   557	We next establish the second part. Given the simplified  polynomial
   558	$X$ as above, define a problem in {\MLT} with the variables
   559	$z_1, z_2, \ldots ,z_n$ as follows. For each
   560	nonzero  term $c_I \prod_{i \in I} x_i$  consider the linear equation
   561	$\sum_{i \in I} z_i =b$, where $b=0$ if $c_I$ is positive, and $b=1$
   562	if $c_I$ is negative, and either associate this equation with the
   563	weight $w_I=|c_I|$, or duplicate it $|c_I|$ times.  It is easy to check
   564	that this system of equations has an assignment $z_i$ satisfying at least
   565	$[\sum_{I \in {\cal S}} w_I+k]/2$ of the equations if and only if
   566	there are $x_i \in \{-1,1\}$ so that $X(x_1,x_2, \ldots ,x_n)
   567	\geq k$. This is shown by the transformation $x_i=(-1)^{z_i}$. See
   568	also \cite{HastadVenkatesh02} and \cite{GutinKimSzeiderYeo09a} for
   569	a similar discussion. Since, as explained above,  we may assume
   570	that $\sum_{I \in {\cal S}} |c_I| =O(k^2)$ (as otherwise  we know
   571	that the answer to our problem is  {\sc Yes}), this provides the
   572	required bikernel of size $O(k^2)$ to {\MLT}.
   573	
   574	It remains to prove the existence of a polynomial size kernel for
   575	the original problem. One way to do that is to apply
   576	Lemma \ref{lem:pk}. Indeed, {\MLT} is in NP, and
   577	{\MST} is NP-complete,
   578	implying the desired  result.
   579	
   580	It is also possible to give a
   581	direct proof, which shows that the problem admits a kernel
   582	of size at most $O(k^2)$. To do so, we replace each linear equation
   583	of at most $r$ variables
   584	by a set of $2^{r-1}$ clauses, so that if the variables $z_i$
   585	satisfy the equation, the corresponding Boolean variables $x_i=(-1)^{z_i}$  satisfy all
   586	these clauses, and if the variables $z_i$ do not satisfy the
   587	equation, then the variables $x_i$ above   satisfy only
   588	$2^{r-1}-1$ of the clauses. This is done as follows.
   589	
   590	Consider, first, a linear equation with exactly $r$ variables.
   591	After
   592	renumbering the variables, if needed,  a typical equation
   593	is of the form  $z_1+z_2 + \cdots +z_r=b$, where the sum is over
   594	$\mathbb{F}_2$ and $b \in \{0,1\}$. There are exactly $2^{r-1}$
   595	Boolean assignments $\delta=(\delta_1,\delta_2, \ldots , \delta_r)$
   596	for the variables $z_i$
   597	that do {\bf not} satisfy  the equation. For each such assignment
   598	$\delta$ let $C_{\delta}$ be the clause consisting of $r$ literals,
   599	where the literal number $i$ is $x_i$ if $\delta_i=1$ and is
   600	$\overline{x_i}$ if $\delta_i=0$. Note that if the variables
   601	$z_1,z_2, \ldots ,z_r$ satisfy the  above equation, then
   602	$(z_1,z_2,\ldots ,z_r)$ is not one of the vectors $\delta$
   603	considered, and hence each of the clauses $C_{\delta}$
   604	constructed contains at
   605	least one satisfied literal when $x_i=(-1)^{z_i}$. Therefore, in this
   606	case all clauses are
   607	satisfied. A similar argument shows that if the variables $z_i$ do
   608	not satisfy the equation, there will  be exactly one non-satisfied
   609	clause, namely the one corresponding to the vector
   610	$\delta=(z_1,z_2, \ldots ,z_r)$. 
   611	
   612	The construction can be extended
   613	to equations with less than $r$ variables. Indeed,
   614	the only property
   615	used in the transformation above is that there are exactly
   616	$2^{r-1}$ Boolean assignments  for the variables  $z_1,z_2, \ldots
   617	,z_r$ that do not satisfy the equation. If the equation
   618	has only $(1 \leq)~ s<r$ variables, add to these variables an arbitrary set
   619	of $r-s$ of the other variables, and consider the set of all
   620	Boolean assignments to this augmented set of variables that do not
   621	satisfy the equation. Here, too, there are exactly $2^{r-1}$ such
   622	assignments  and we can thus repeat the construction above in this
   623	case as well.
   624	
   625	The above procedure transforms a set of $W$ linear equations over
   626	$\mathbb{F}_2$
   627	into a multiset of $2^{r-1} W$ clauses. Moreover, if some truth
   628	assignment  does not satisfy exactly $\ell$ equations, then the same
   629	assignment does not satisfy the same number, $\ell$, of clauses.
   630	In particular, there is an assignment satisfying all equations
   631	but $(W-k)/2$ of them if and only if  there is an assignment
   632	satisfying all clauses but $(W-k)/2$ of them. This means that
   633	among
   634	the $m=2^{r-1}W$ clauses, the number of satisfied ones is
   635	$m-(W-k)/2=[(2^r-1)m+2^{r-1}k]/2^r$. This reduces an instance of
   636	{\MLT} with $W$ equations and parameter $k$ to an instance of {\MST}
   637	with $2^{r-1}W$ clauses and parameter $2^{r-1}k$. Since $r$ is a
   638	constant, this provides the required kernel of size $O(k^2)$,
   639	completing the proof.
   640	\iffalse
   641	We start from (i).
   642	Consider {\MLT} and let an equation $e_j$ be $\sum_{i\in I_j}z_i=b_j.$
   643	Consider the following random variable first introduced
   644	in~\cite{HastadVenkatesh02}:
   645	$Y=\sum_{j=1}^m Y_j$, where
   646	$Y_j=(-1)^{b_j} w_j \prod_{i \in I_j} y_i$ and all $y_i$ are independent
   647	uniform random variables on $\{-1,1\}$. By \cite{GutinKimSzeiderYeo09a}, the answer to {\MLT} is {\sc Yes} if and only if $Y\ge 2k$
   648	for some assignment of values to $y_1,\ldots ,y_n$.
   649	
   650	Now observe that  the simplified $X$ given just after Lemma \ref{lem0}
   651	can be viewed as $Y$, where $c_I=(-1)^{b_I} w_I$, $w_I=|c_I|$, and
   652	$b_I=0$ if $c_I>0$ and $b_I=1$, otherwise. So, the simplified $X$ corresponds to {\MLT}.
   653	Thus, in the first part of this proof we have obtained a polynomial-size bikernel from {\MST} to {\MLT}.
   654	
   655	To prove (ii) observe that {\sc Max $r$-Lin2} is in NP.
   656	Thus, by Lemma \ref{lem:pk}, (ii) follows from (i).
   657	\fi
   658	\end{proof}
   659	
   660	Our algorithm for the problem $\MST$ can be easily modified to provide, efficiently, for any given
   661	instance of $m$ clauses to which there is a truth assignment satisfying
   662	at least $k/2^r$ clauses above the average, an assignment for the
   663	variables with this property.  Indeed, the proof of Theorem~\ref{t95}
   664	only requires that the variables $x_i$ are $4r$-wise independent, and
   665	there are known constructions of polynomial size sample spaces
   666	supporting such random variables (see, e.g., \cite{AS}, Chapter 16).
   667	Thus, if in the polynomial $X$, $\sqrt{|{\cal S}|}/(2\cdot 3^r)\geq k$,
   668	then one can find an assignment satisfying at least as many clauses as
   669	needed by going over all points in such a sample space, and if $\sqrt{|{\cal S}|}/(2\cdot 3^r)< k$, one
   670	can solve the problem by an exhaustive search.
   671	
   672	%We mention in passing that the hidden constant in the number of variables $4\cdot 9^rk^2$ adds to the exponent in the running time of our algorithm.
   673	
   674	

