   675	\section{MAX-$2$-SAT}\label{sec4}
   676	
   677	In this section we describe an alternative, more combinatorial, approach
   678	to the problem $\MST$ for $r=2$. Although this approach is somewhat more
   679	complicated than the one discussed in the previous section, it provides
   680	an additional insight to this special case of the problem, and allows us
   681	to obtain a kernel with a linear number of variables for \textsc{Max-$2$-Sat${}_{\text{tlb}}$}.
   682	
   683	We start with a simple reduction rule that applies to any value of~$r$.
   684	
   685	
   686	
   687	\subsection{The Semicomplete Data Reduction}
   688	
   689	Several data reduction rules, or preprocessing methods in a broader sense, have been suggested and applied to {\sc Max-Sat} in the literature (cf. \cite{SZ05} and references therein). We are especially interested in reduction rules which simplify 2-CNF formulas. Most preprocessing methods for 2-CNF formulas are not applicable to our type of parameterization. For example, a data reduction rule known as {\em Resolution Rule}, replaces two clauses $xy$, $\ol{x}z$ by $yz$ if the literals $x$ or $\ol{x}$ does not appear in any other clauses. Resolution Rule is sound under the parameterization of {\sc Max-2-Sat}, where we ask if all except for at most $k$ clauses can be satisfied. However, this reduction rule is not applicable to our type of parameterization. On the other hand, there is a data reduction rule introduced in \cite{SZ05}, where it was called {\em Complementary Unit Rule}, which is applicable to our parameterization of  {\sc Max-2-Sat}. Below we use this reduction rule.
   690	
   691	We say that a pair of distinct clauses $Y$ and $Z$ has a \emph{conflict}
   692	if there is a literal $p\in Y$ such that $\ol{p}\in Z$. We say that an
   693	$r$-CNF formula $F$ is \emph{semicomplete} if the number of clauses is
   694	$m=2^r$ and every pair of distinct clauses of $F$ has a conflict.  A
   695	semicomplete $r$-CNF formula is \emph{complete} if each clause is over
   696	the same set of variables.  There are $r$-CNF formulas that are
   697	semicomplete but not complete; consider for example $\{x y$, $x\ol{y}$,
   698	$\ol{x}z$, $\ol{x} \ol{z}\}$. We have the following:
   699	
   700	\begin{lemma}\label{lem:semic}
   701	  Every truth assignment to a semicomplete $r$-CNF formula satisfies
   702	  exactly $2^r-1$ clauses.
   703	\end{lemma}
   704	
   705	\begin{proof}
   706	  Let $S$ be a semicomplete $r$-CNF formula.  To prove that no truth
   707	  assignment satisfies all clauses of $S$ we use the following simple
   708	  counting argument from~\cite{Iwama89}.  Observe that every clause is
   709	  not satisfied by exactly $2^{n-r}$ truth assignments. However, each of
   710	  these assignments satisfies each other clause (due to the
   711	  conflicts). So, we have exactly $2^r \cdot 2^{n-r}$ truth assignments
   712	  not satisfying $S$. But $2^r \cdot 2^{n-r}=2^n$, the total number of
   713	  truth assignments.
   714	
   715	  Now let $\tau$ be a truth assignment of $S$. By the above, $\tau$ does
   716	  not satisfy a clause $C$ of $S$. However, $\tau$ satisfies any other
   717	  clause of $S$ as any other clause has a conflict with~$C$.
   718	\end{proof}
   719	
   720	\noindent Consider the following data reduction procedure.
   721	
   722	Given an $r$-CNF formula $F$ that contains a semicomplete subset
   723	$F'\subseteq F$, delete $F'$ from $F$ and consider $F\setminus F'$
   724	instead.  Let $F^S$ denote the formula obtained from $F$ by applying
   725	this deletion process as long as possible.  We say that $F^S$ is
   726	obtained from $F$ by \emph{semicomplete reduction}.
   727	
   728	We state the following two simple observations as a lemma.
   729	
   730	\begin{lemma}\label{lem:redsafe}
   731	  Let $F$ be an $r$-CNF formula.
   732	  \begin{enumerate}
   733	  \item  $F^S$ can be obtained from $F$ in polynomial time.
   734	  \item $\sat(F)-\sat(F^S)=(1-2^{-r})(\Card{F}-\Card{F^S})$.
   735	  \end{enumerate}
   736	\end{lemma}
   737	
   738	
   739	\subsection{Kernelization}
   740	
   741	Let $F$ be a 2-CNF formula.  A variable $x\in \var(F)$ is
   742	\emph{insignificant} if for each literal $y$ the numbers of occurrences
   743	of the two clauses $xy$ and $\ol{x}y$ in $F$ are the same. A variable
   744	$x\in \var(F)$ is \emph{significant} if it is not insignificant. A
   745	literal is significant or insignificant if its underlying variable is
   746	significant or insignificant, respectively.
   747	\begin{theorem}\label{the:sigkernel}
   748	  Let $F$ be a 2-CNF formula with $F=F^S$ (i.e., $F$ contains no
   749	  semicomplete subsets) and let $k\geq 0$ be an integer. If $F$ has more
   750	  than $3k-2$ significant variables, then $\sat(F)\geq (3\Card{F}+k)/4$.
   751	\end{theorem}
   752	
   753	The remainder of this section is devoted to the proof of
   754	Theorem~\ref{the:sigkernel} and its corollary. Let $F$ be a 2-CNF formula with $m$ clauses and $n$ variables and let $k$ be an integer. We assume that $F$ contains no semicomplete subsets, i.e., $F=F^S$.
   755	
   756	For a literal $x$ let $c(x)$ denote the number of clauses in $F$
   757	containing $x$. Given a pair of literals $x$ and $y$, $x\neq \ol{y}$,
   758	let $c(xy)$ be the number of occurrences of clause $xy$ in~$F$.
   759	
   760	Given a clause $C\in F$ and a variable $x\in \var(F)$, let $\delta_C(x)$
   761	be an indicator variable whose value is set as $\delta_C(x)=1$ if $x\in
   762	C$, $\delta_C(x)=-1$ if $\ol{x}\in C$, and $\delta_C(x)=0$ otherwise.
   763	
   764	\begin{lemma}\label{lem:above}
   765	  For each subset $R=\{x_1,\ldots ,x_q\}\subseteq \var(F)$ we have
   766	  $\sat(F)\geq (3m +k_R)/4$ for
   767	  \[
   768	  k_R= \sum_{1\leq i \leq q} (c(x_i)-c(\ol{x_i})) +
   769	  \sum_{1\leq i < j \leq q}
   770	  \big(
   771	 c(x_i\ol{x_j})+c(\ol{x_i}x_j)
   772	  -c(x_ix_j)-c(\ol{x_i}\ol{x_j})
   773	  \big).
   774	\]
   775	\end{lemma}
   776	\begin{proof}
   777	  Take a random truth assignment $\tau\in 2^{\var(F)}$ such that
   778	  $\tau(x_i)=1$ for all $i\in \{1,\dots,q\}$ and
   779	  $\Prob(\tau(x)=1)=0.5$ for all $x\in \var(F)\setminus R$. A simple
   780	  case analysis yields that the probability that a clause $C\in F$ is
   781	  satisfied by $\tau$ is given by
   782	  \[
   783	  \Prob(\text{$\tau$ satisfies $C$})=1-\frac{1}{4}\prod_{1\leq i
   784	    \leq q}(1-\delta_C(x_i)).
   785	  \]
   786	  \sloppypar Observe that for any clause $C$ and any three distinct
   787	  variables $x,y,z$ we have $\delta_C(x) \delta_C(y)\delta_C(z)=0$ as
   788	  $\var(C)$ contains exactly two variables.  Hence we can determine the
   789	  expected number of clauses satisfied by $\tau$ as follows.
   790	\begin{eqnarray*}
   791	  \Exp(\sat(\tau,F))
   792	  &=&
   793	  \sum_{C\in F} \Prob[\;\text{$\tau$ satisfies $C$}\;]\\
   794	  &=&
   795	  \sum_{C\in F}\big\{1-\frac{1}{4}\prod_{1\leq i \leq q}(1-\delta_C(x_i))\big\} \\
   796	  &=&
   797	  \frac{3}{4}m +
   798	  \frac{1}{4}
   799	  \sum_{C\in F}\big\{
   800	  \sum_{1\leq i \leq q} \delta_C(x_i)
   801	  -\sum_{1\leq i < j \leq q}\delta_C(x_i)\delta_C(x_j)\big\} \\
   802	  &=&
   803	  \frac{3}{4}m +
   804	  \frac{1}{4} \big\{
   805	  \sum_{1\leq i \leq q} \sum_{C \in F} \delta_C(x_i)
   806	  - \sum_{1\leq i < j \leq q} \sum_{C \in F}\delta_C(x_i)\delta_C(x_j)\big\}  \\
   807	%   & =&
   808	%   \frac{3}{4}m +
   809	%   \frac{1}{4}\big\{
   810	%   \sum_{1\leq i \leq q} (c(x_i)-c(\ol{x_i}))
   811	%   - \!\!\!\sum_{1\leq i < j \leq q}\!\! (
   812	%   \epsilon(x_ix_j)+\epsilon(\ol{x_i}\ol{x_j})
   813	%   -\epsilon(x_i\ol{x_j})-\epsilon(\ol{x_i}x_j)\big\}.
   814	  & =&
   815	  \frac{3}{4}m +
   816	  \frac{1}{4}k_R. \hspace{7cm}\qedhere
   817	\end{eqnarray*}
   818	\end{proof}
   819	
   820	\medskip\noindent It is noteworthy that $\Prob(\text{$\tau$ satisfies $C$})=1-\frac{1}{4}\prod_{1\leq i \leq q}(1-\delta_C(x_i))$ in the proof of Lemma \ref{lem:above} is similar to a term of $X$ defined in Section \ref{algerep}. The term $1-\prod_{x_i\in \var(C)}(1+\epsilon_ix_i)$ of $X$ returns a fixed value on $C$ for a given ({\em fully determined}) truth assignment, depending on whether $C$ is satisfied or not. Similarly, the term $1-\frac{1}{4}\prod_{1\leq i \leq q}(1-\delta_C(x_i))$ returns a probability of $C$ being satisfied for a given ({\em partially determined}) random truth assignment. The benefit of having a probabilistic form of $X$ is that we now have a way to ignore a large number of variables, e.g., $V\setminus R$ in Lemma \ref{lem:above}, instead of searching for a fully determined truth assignment so as to compute $X$. For the case $r=2$, this probabilistic form of $X$ can be immediately interpreted in a graph-theoretical language as will be shown below.
   821	
   822	Due to Lemma \ref{lem:above}, the task is now reduced into finding a subset $R$ of variables with $k_R\geq k$. These are variables which form the deterministic part of a partially random truth assignment. Using a notion of {\em switch} defined later, we replace $F$ by an equivalent formula in which every variable of $R$ is set to 1.
   823	To find $R$ we use a graph-theoretical approach introducing an auxiliary weighted graph in which we seek an induced subgraph of weight at least $k$.
   824	In particular, we note that an `independent' structure of an induced subgraph ensures its weight to be above a certain bound growing with the size of the induced subgraph. This means that if $(F,k)$ is a {\sc No}-instance, we do not have a large `independent' structure. Using  this fact and the Tutte-Berge formula for the size of a maximum matching in a graph (stated after Lemma \ref{collectionstar}), we will prove an upper bound on the number of vertices in the auxiliary weighted graph.
   825	
   826	
   827	
   828	\medskip\noindent We construct an \emph{auxiliary graph} $G=(V,E)$ from
   829	$F$ by letting $V=\var(F)$ and $xy\in E$ if and only if there exists a
   830	clause $C\in F$ with $\var(C)=\{x,y\}$ (equivalently, $c(x \ol{y}) +
   831	c(\ol{x}y) + c(xy) + c(\ol{x} \ol{y})\geq 1$).
   832	
   833	We assign a \emph{weight} to each vertex $x$ and edge $xy$ of
   834	$G=(V,E)$:
   835	\begin{eqnarray*}
   836	  w(x)&:=&\sum_{C \in F} \delta_C(x)=c(x)-c(\ol{x}), \\
   837	  w(xy)&:=& -\sum_{C \in F}\delta_C(x)\delta_C(y)
   838	  =c(x \ol{y}) + c(\ol{x}y)
   839	  - c(xy) - c(\ol{x} \ol{y}).
   840	\end{eqnarray*}
   841	For subsets $U\subseteq V$ and $H\subseteq E$, let $w(U)=\sum_{x\in
   842	  U}w(x)$ and $w(H)=\sum_{xy\in H}w(xy)$. The \emph{weight} $w(Q)$ of a
   843	subgraph $Q=(U,H)$ is $w(U)+w(H)$. Let $G^0$ be the graph obtained from
   844	$G$ by removing all edges of weight zero.
   845	\begin{lemma}\label{isol}
   846	  A variable $x\in \var(F)$ is insignificant if and only if $x$ is an
   847	  isolated vertex in $G^0$ and $w(x)=0$.
   848	\end{lemma}
   849	\begin{proof}
   850	  Suppose $x\in \var(F)$ is insignificant.  Choose an edge $xy\in E$
   851	  (this is possible since by construction $G$ has no isolated vertices).
   852	  Since $x$ is insignificant, $c(x \ol{y})=c(\ol{x} \ol{y})$ and $c(xy)
   853	  = c(\ol{x}y)$ and thus $w(xy)=0$. Therefore the edge $xy$ does not
   854	  appear in $G^0$ and $x$ is isolated in $G^0$. Observe that we have
   855	  $c(x)=c(\ol{x})$, which implies $w(x)=0$.
   856	
   857	  Suppose $x\in \var(F)$ is an isolated vertex of $G^0$ and
   858	  $w(x)=0$. Since $G$ has no isolated vertices, we have $w(xy)=0$ for
   859	  all $xy\in E$. In order to derive a contradiction, let us suppose $x$
   860	  is a significant variable of $F$. Consequently there is (i)~either a
   861	  clause $xy\in F$ such that $c(xy)>c(\ol{x}y)$, or (ii)~there is a
   862	  clause $\ol{x}y\in F$ such that $c(\ol{x}y)>c(xy)$. We consider
   863	  case~(i) only, case~(ii) can be treated analogously.  With $w(xy)= 0$,
   864	  we have $c(x\ol{y})>c(\ol{x}\ol{y})$, and thus $x\ol{y}\in F$.
   865	
   866	  Now the condition $w(x)=c(x)-c(\ol{x})=0$ implies the existence of an
   867	  edge $xz\in E$ with $z\neq y$ such that for some $z'\in \{z,\ol{z}\}$
   868	  we have $\ol{x}z'\in F$ and $c(\ol{x}z')>c(xz')$. Without loss of
   869	  generality, assume that $z'=z$. Since $w(xz)=0$, we have
   870	  $\ol{x}\ol{z}\in F$. However, the four clauses $xy$, $x\ol{y}$,
   871	  $\ol{x}z$, $\ol{x}\ol{z}$~in $F$ form a semicomplete 2-CNF formula,
   872	  which contradicts our assumption that $F=F^S$.  Hence $x$ is indeed an
   873	  insignificant variable.
   874	\end{proof}
   875	
   876	
   877	For a set $X \subseteq \var(F)$ we let $F_X$ denote the 2-CNF formula
   878	obtained from $F$ by replacing $x$ with $\ol{x}$ and $\ol{x}$ with $x$
   879	for each $x\in X$. We say that $F_X$ is obtained from $F$ by
   880	\emph{switching}~$X$.
   881	
   882	
   883	The following lemma follows immediately from the definitions of switch
   884	and weights.
   885	
   886	\begin{lemma}\label{lem:switch}
   887	  The auxiliary graph $G_X$ corresponding to $F_X$ can be obtained from
   888	  $G=(V,E)$ by reversing the signs of the weights of all vertices in $X$
   889	  and all edges between $X$ and $V\setminus X$.  Moreover,
   890	  $\sat(F)=\sat(F_X)$.
   891	\end{lemma}
   892	
   893	
   894	To distinguish between weights in $G$ and $G_X$, we use $w_X(.)$ for
   895	weights of $G_X$. Similarly, we use $c_X(.)$ for~$F_X$.
   896	
   897	It is sometimes convenient to stress that the set $X$ we are switching
   898	induces a subgraph. We can \emph{switch an induced graph} $Q$ by
   899	switching all the vertices of~$Q$. Observe that by switching an induced
   900	graph $Q$, we reverse the signs of weights on all vertices of $Q$ and
   901	all edges incident with exactly one vertex of $Q$, but the sign of each
   902	edge within $Q$ remains unchanged. This property will play a major role
   903	to show that a certain structure meets the condition of the following
   904	lemma.
   905	
   906	
   907	
   908	\begin{lemma}\label{lem:subgraph}
   909	  If there exist a set $X\subset V(G^0)$ and an induced subgraph
   910	  $Q=(U,H)$ of $G^0$ with $w_X(Q)\geq k$, then $\sat(F)\geq (3m+k)/4$.
   911	\end{lemma}
   912	\begin{proof}
   913	  We consider $U=\{x_1,\dots,x_q\}$ as a subset of $\var(F_X)$. By
   914	  Lemmas~\ref{lem:above} and~\ref{lem:switch}, $\sat(F)=\sat(F_X)\geq
   915	  (3m +k_U)/4$, where
   916	  \begin{eqnarray*}
   917	 k_U \!\!&=&\!\!  \sum_{i=1}^q (c_X(x_i)-c_X(\ol{x_i})) +
   918	     \!\! \sum_{1\leq i < j \leq q}(
   919	     c_X(x_i \ol{x_j}) + c_X(\ol{x_i}x_j)
   920	     - c_X(x_ix_j) - c_X(\ol{x_i} \ol{x_j}) )  \\
   921	   &=&\!\! \sum_{i=1}^q w_X(x_i) + \sum_{1\leq i < j \leq q}w_X(x_ix_j)
   922	   \quad = \quad w_X(Q)  \quad\geq\quad k.
   923	\hspace{0.4cm}\qedhere
   924	  \end{eqnarray*}
   925	\end{proof}
   926	
   927	To apply Lemma~\ref{lem:subgraph} in the proof of
   928	Theorem~\ref{the:sigkernel}, we will focus on a special case of induced
   929	subgraphs of $G^0$. For a set $U\subseteq V(G^0)$, let $G^0[U]$ denote
   930	the subgraph of $G^0$ induced by $U$. We call $G^0[U]$ an {\em induced
   931	  star} with {\em center} $x$ if $x$ is a vertex of $G^0$, $I$ is an
   932	independent set in the subgraph of $G^0$ induced by the neighbors of $x$
   933	and $U=\{x\}\cup I$. We are interested in the induced star due to the
   934	following property.
   935	
   936	\begin{lemma}\label{inducedstar}
   937	  Let $x$ be the center of an induced star $Q=G^0[U]$ and let
   938	  $I=U\setminus \{x\}$.  Then there is a set $X\subseteq U$ such that
   939	  $w_X(Q)\ge |I|$.
   940	\end{lemma}
   941	\begin{proof}
   942	  Let $H$ be the set of edges of $Q$. We may assume that $w(xy)\geq 0$ for
   943	  each $y \in I$ since otherwise we can switch $y$, and $w(xy$) is integral. By a \emph{random
   944	    switch} of $Q$, we mean a switch of every vertex of $Q$ with probability $0.5$. Take
   945	  a random switch $R$ of~$Q$. Then we have $\Exp(w_R(z))=0$ for all
   946	  $z\in U$. Note that the sign of each edge in $H$ remains
   947	  positive. Hence we have $\Exp(w_R(Q))= w(H) \geq |I|$ and thus there
   948	  exists a set $X\subseteq U$ for which $w_X(Q)\geq |I|$.
   949	\end{proof}
   950	
   951	If we are given more than one induced star, a sequence of random
   952	switches gives us a similar result.
   953	
   954	\begin{lemma}\label{collectionstar}
   955	  Let $Q_1=(U_1,H_1),\ldots ,Q_m=(U_m,H_m)$ be a collection of
   956	  vertex-disjoint induced stars of $G^0$ with centers $x_1,\ldots ,x_m$,
   957	  let $U=\bigcup_{i=1}^{m}U_i$, and let $Q=G^0[U]$.  Then there is a set
   958	  $X\subseteq U$ such that $w_X(Q)\ge \sum_{i=1}^m |I_i|,$ where
   959	  $I_i=U_i\setminus \{x_i\},$ $i=1,\ldots ,m$.
   960	\end{lemma}
   961	\begin{proof}
   962	  As in the proof of Lemma~\ref{inducedstar}, we may assume that all the
   963	  edges of $H_i$ have positive weights.  Let $H$ be the set of edges of
   964	  $Q$.  By a \emph{random switch} of $Q$, we mean a sequence of switches
   965	  of $Q_1,\ldots ,Q_m$ each with probability $0.5$. Take a random switch
   966	  $R$ of~$Q$. Then we have $\Exp(w_R(x))=0$ for all $x\in U$. Moreover,
   967	  for the subgraph $Q$ of $G^0_R$, it holds that $\Exp(w_R(xy))=0$ for
   968	  all $xy\in H\setminus \bigcup_{i=1}^{m}H_i$ since each choice of
   969	  $w_R(xy)\geq 0$ and $w_R(xy)\leq 0$ is equally likely. By linearity of
   970	  expectation and Lemma~\ref{inducedstar}, we have $\Exp(w_R(Q))=
   971	  w(\bigcup_{i=1}^{m}H_i) \geq \sum_{i=1}^m |I_i|$ and thus there exists
   972	  a set $X\subseteq U$ for which $w_X(Q)\geq \sum_{i=1}^m |I_i|$.
   973	\end{proof}
   974	
   975	Note that we can derandomize the procedures suggested in the proofs of Lemma \ref{inducedstar} and \ref{collectionstar} using the standard technique of conditional expectation \cite{AS}.
   976	%In fact, the derandomization the former is even simpler: compare $w(Q)$ and $w_U(Q)$, then choose the one with the larger weight.
   977	%\begin{lemma}\label{indep}
   978	%If there is an independent set $I$ in $G^0$ with $|I|\geq k$ and $w(x)\neq 0$ for all $x\in I$, then the answer to the instance $\mc{F}$ with $k$ is {\sc yes}.
   979	%\end{lemma}
   980	%\begin{proof}
   981	%We may assume that $w(x)>0$ for every vertex $x\in I$ since otherwise we can switch the corresponding variable in $\mc{F}$. Let $V'=I$ and $E'=\emptyset$. Then $(V',E')$ is an induced subgraph of $G^0$ with $w(V')+w(E')\geq k$ and we can apply Lemma \ref{subgraph}.
   982	%\end{proof}
   983	
   984	We are now in the position to complete the proof of
   985	Theorem~\ref{the:sigkernel}.
   986	
   987	Suppose that $(F,k)$ is a no-instance, i.e., $\sat(F)< (3m+k)/4$.
   988	Notice that a matching can be viewed as a collection of induced stars of
   989	$G^0$ for which $|I_i|=1$. It follows by Lemmas~\ref{lem:subgraph} and
   990	\ref{collectionstar} that $G^0$ has no matching of size $k$.  The
   991	Tutte-Berge formula \cite{Berge58,BondyMurty08} states that the size of
   992	a maximum matching in $G^0$ equals
   993	\[
   994	\min_{S\subseteq V(G^0)}\frac{1}{2}\{ |V(G^0)| + |S| - oc(G^0-S)\}
   995	\]
   996	where $oc(G^0-S)$ is the number of odd components (connected components
   997	with an odd number of vertices) in $G^0-S$. Hence there is a set
   998	$S\subseteq V(G^0)$ such that $|V(G^0)| + |S| - oc(G^0-S) < 2k$. It
   999	follows that
  1000	\begin{equation}\label{eq1}
  1001	  |V(G^0)|\le oc(G^0-S)-|S|+2k-1.
  1002	\end{equation}
  1003	
  1004	
  1005	We will now classify odd components in $G^0-S$. One obvious type of odd
  1006	components is an isolated vertex in $G^0$ of weight zero, which
  1007	corresponds to an insignificant variable by Lemma~\ref{isol}. All the
  1008	other odd components can be categorized into one of the following two
  1009	types:
  1010	
  1011	\begin{enumerate}
  1012	\item Let $Q_1,\ldots, Q_L$ be the odd components of $G^0-S$ such that
  1013	  for all $1\leq i \leq L$ we have $|Q_i|=1$ and $Q_i$ is a significant
  1014	  variable.
  1015	
  1016	\item Let $Q'_1,\ldots, Q'_{L'}$ be the odd components of $G^0-S$ such
  1017	  that for all $1\leq i \leq L'$ we have $|Q'_i|>1$.
  1018	\end{enumerate}
  1019	
  1020	We construct a collection of induced stars as follows. From each of
  1021	$Q'_1,\ldots, Q'_{L'}$ we choose an edge, which is an induced star with
  1022	$|I|=1$. Let us consider $Q_1,\ldots, Q_L$. Each vertex $Q_i$ is
  1023	adjacent to at least one vertex of $S$. Thus, we can partition
  1024	$Q_1,\ldots, Q_L$ into $|S|$ sets, some of them possibly empty, such
  1025	that each partite set forms an independent set in which every vertex is
  1026	adjacent to the corresponding vertex $x_i$ of $S$. Each partite set,
  1027	together with $x_i$, forms an induced star. Now observe that we have a
  1028	collection of induced stars and the total number of edges equals
  1029	$L+L'$. If $L+L' \ge k$, Lemma~\ref{collectionstar} implies that for
  1030	some set $X$ of vertices from the odd components $w_X(Q)\geq k$, which
  1031	is impossible by Lemma~\ref{lem:subgraph}. Hence $L+L'\leq k-1$.
  1032	
  1033	Therefore, $oc(G^0-S)-n' = L + L' \leq k-1$,
  1034	where $n'$ is the number of insignificant variables. By (\ref{eq1}),
  1035	we have $|V(G^0)|-n'\le k-1-|S|+2k-1\le 3k-2$. It remains to observe that $|V(G^0)|-n'$ equals the number of
  1036	significant variables of~$F$. This completes the proof of
  1037	Theorem~\ref{the:sigkernel}.
  1038	
  1039	
  1040	
  1041	\begin{corollary}\label{the:kern}
  1042	  The problem {\MTT} admits a (polynomial time) reduction to a problem
  1043	  kernel with at most $3k-1$ variables.
  1044	\end{corollary}
  1045	\begin{proof}
  1046	  Consider an instance $(F,k)$ of the problem.  First we apply the
  1047	  semicomplete reduction and obtain (in polynomial time) an instance
  1048	  $(F',k)$ with $F'=F^S$. We determine (again in polynomial time) the
  1049	  set $S'$ of significant variables of $F'$. If $\Card{S'}> 3k-2$ then
  1050	  $(F',k)$ is a yes-instance by Theorem~\ref{the:sigkernel}, and
  1051	  consequently $(F,k)$ is a yes-instance by Lemma~\ref{lem:redsafe}.
  1052	  Assume now that $\Card{S'}\leq 3k-2$.
  1053	
  1054	  Let $z$ be a new variable not occurring in $F$. Since $F'=F^S$, no
  1055	  clause contains two insignificant variables and, thus, each
  1056	  insignificant variable can be replaced by $z$ without changing the
  1057	  solution to $(F',k)$. Let us denote the modified $F'$ by $F''$; $F''$
  1058	  has at most $3k-1$ variables.
  1059	
  1060	  Let $p$ be the number of clauses in $F''$. Observe that we can find a
  1061	  truth assignment satisfying the maximum number of clauses of $F''$ in
  1062	  time $O(p8^k)$. Thus, if $p>8^k$, we can find the optimal truth
  1063	  assignment in the polynomial time $O(p^2)=O(m^2)$. Thus, we may assume
  1064	  that $F''$ has at most $8^k$ clauses. Therefore, $F''$ is a kernel of
  1065	  the {\MTT} problem.
  1066	\end{proof}
  1067	
  1068	
  1069	\section{Extension to Boolean Constraint Satisfaction Problems}\label{sec5}
  1070	
  1069	\section{Extension to Boolean Constraint Satisfaction Problems}\label{sec5}
  1070	
  1071	
  1072	The fixed-parameter tractability result on {\MST}
  1073	can be easily extended to any family of Boolean $r$-Constraint
  1074	Satisfaction Problems.  Here is an outline of the argument.
  1075	
  1076	Let $r$ be a fixed positive integer, let
  1077	$\Phi$ be a set of Boolean functions, each involving at
  1078	most $r$ variables, and let
  1079	${\cal F}=\{f_1, f_2,
  1080	\ldots, f_m\}$ be a collection of Boolean functions, each being a
  1081	member of $\Phi$, and each acting on some subset of
  1082	the $n$ Boolean variables $x_1,x_2, \ldots ,x_n$.
  1083	The Boolean Max-$r$-Constraint
  1084	Satisfaction Problem (corresponding to $\Phi$), which we denote by
  1085	the {\sc Max-$r$-CSP} problem, for short, when $\Phi$ is clear from
  1086	the context, is the
  1087	problem of finding a truth assignment to the variables so as to maximize
  1088	the total number of functions satisfied. Note that this includes, as a
  1089	special case, the \textsc{Max-$r$-Sat} problem considered in the
  1090	previous section, as well as many related problems.  As most interesting
  1091	problems of this type are NP-hard, we consider their parameterized
  1092	version, where the parameter is, as before, the number of functions
  1093	satisfied minus the expected value of this number.  Note, in
  1094	passing, that the above expected value is a tight lower
  1095	bound for the problem, whenever the family ${\Phi}$ is closed under
  1096	replacing each variable by its complement, since if we apply any
  1097	Boolean function to all $2^r$ choices of literals whose underlying
  1098	variables are any fixed set of $r$ variables, then any truth
  1099	assignment  to the variables satisfies exactly the same number of
  1100	these $2^r$ functions.
  1101	
  1102	For each Boolean
  1103	function $f$ of $r(f)$ Boolean variables
  1104	\[
  1105	x_{i_1}, x_{i_2}, \ldots , x_{i_{r(f)}},
  1106	\]
  1107	define a random variable $X_f$ as follows. As in the discussion of the
  1108	\textsc{Max-$r$-Sat} problem, suppose each variable $x_{i_j}$ attains
  1109	values in $\{-1,1\}$.  Let $V \subseteq \{-1,1\}^{r(f)}$ denote the set of all
  1110	satisfying assignments of $f$. Then
  1111	\[
  1112	X_f(x_1, x_2, \ldots ,x_n) = \sum_{v=(v_1, \ldots ,v_{r(f)}) \in V}
  1113	2^{r-r(f)}[\prod_{j=1}^{r(f)} (1+x_{i_j} v_j) - 1].
  1114	\]
  1115	
  1116	
  1117	This is a random variable defined over the space $\{-1,1\}^n$ and its
  1118	value at $x=(x_1,x_2, \ldots ,x_n)$ is $2^r-|V|\cdot 2^{r-r(f)}$ if $x$ satisfies $f$,
  1119	and is $-|V|\cdot 2^{r-r(f)}$ otherwise.  Thus, the expectation of $X_f$ is zero. Define
  1120	now $X =\sum_{f \in {\cal F}} X_f$.  Then the value of $X$ at $x=(x_1,
  1121	x_2, \ldots ,x_n)$ is precisely $2^r(s-a)$, where $s$ is the number of
  1122	the functions satisfied by the truth assignment $x$, and $a$ is the
  1123	average value of the number of satisfied functions.  Our objective is to
  1124	decide if $X$ attains a value of at least $k$. As this is a polynomial
  1125	of degree at most $r$ with integer coefficients and expectation zero, we
  1126	can repeat the arguments of Section~\ref{sec3} and prove that, for every
  1127	fixed $r$, the problem is fixed-parameter tractable.
  1128	Moreover, our previous arguments show that the problem admits
  1129	a polynomial-size bikernel reducing it to  an instance of {\MLT} of
  1130	size $O(k^2)$, and if the specific $r$-CSP problem considered is
  1131	NP-complete, then there is a polynomial
  1132	size kernel. This is the case for most interesting choices of
  1133	the family ${\Phi}$.
  1134	
  1135	\section{Discussions}\label{sec:d}
  1136	
  1137	Our results are mainly of theoretical interest. However, our kenelization for $\MTT$ might be of some practical interest for families of istances of \textsc{Max-$2$-Sat} where the maximum number of satisfied clauses is close to $3m/4.$
  1138	
  1139	Recently Crowston et al. \cite{CGJKR} proved that $\MTT$ has a kernel with $O(k \log k)$ variables for every fixed $r\ge 2$. The new result uses several ideas given in this paper, but employes linear algebraic rather than probabilistic tools.
  1140	
  1141	\medskip
  1142	\paragraph{Acknowledgments}
  1143	Research of Alon was partially supported by an ERC Advanced grant.
  1144	Research of Gutin, Kim and Yeo was supported in part by an EPSRC
  1145	grant. Research of Gutin was also supported

