-## What this family asks
+## What the problem is
+
+[Alon et al., *Solving MAX-r-SAT Above a Tight Lower Bound*](https://arxiv.org/abs/0907.4573) defines an exact (r)-CNF as a multiset of clauses containing exactly (r) distinct literals. For (r=3), (m) clauses, and scaled surplus (k), the task is to satisfy at least ((7m+k)/8) clauses. This family sets (k=m), so the witness must satisfy every displayed clause.
+
+The solver receives the signed 3-CNF itself and returns one bit per variable. The verifier checks shape, evaluates every literal, counts satisfied clauses exactly, and compares with the integer target. It never reads the planted answer.
 
-[Alon, Gutin, Kim, Szeider, and Yeo, *Solving MAX-r-SAT Above a Tight Lower Bound*](https://arxiv.org/abs/0907.4573) defines an exact (r)-CNF as a multiset of clauses with exactly (r) distinct literals per clause. For (r=3), an instance with (m) clauses and parameter (k) asks for an assignment satisfying at least ((7m+k)/8) clauses. This generator sets (k=m), so a witness must satisfy all clauses. The solver receives the signed 3-CNF clauses themselves and returns one bit per variable. `verify` evaluates every literal and performs the exact integer threshold comparison; it never reads the planted answer.
+The certificate is known by inverse generation. A random assignment is sampled first and evaluated under an invertible binary operator (A=I+N), where (N^2=0), hence (A^2=I). Each resulting three-variable parity equation is converted to the four ordinary clauses specified in the paper. Same-format dependent parity rows add crowding without changing the unique solution.
 
-The certificate is known by inverse generation. The generator first samples an assignment, evaluates a coupled system of three-variable parity equations, and converts every parity row to the four exact 3-clauses displayed in the direct proof of Section 3, Theorem 1. Its binary row matrix is (A=I+N) with (N^2=0), so (A^2=I) and the core has exactly one solution. Complementary decoy-clause pairs are then added without invalidating that solution, and all variables and clauses are relabelled or shuffled.
+## Why it is Track B
 
-## Why Track B, and not Track A
+This is not a Track-A distributional-hardness claim. Theorem 1 gives a general (O(m)+2^{O(k^2)}) algorithm and an (O(k^2)) kernel; the paragraph after its proof explains witness recovery by a bounded-independence sample space or exhaustive search. Thus small (k) is an easy regime and was deliberately avoided. Section 5 gives an even sharper (3k-1)-variable kernel for reduced Max-2-SAT, so this family uses (r=3).
 
-The paper does **not** prove distributional hardness for randomly planted clauses. Section 3, Theorem 1 instead gives an (O(m)+2^{O(k^2)}) algorithm and an (O(k^2)) kernel; it also explains how to recover a witness through a (4r)-wise independent sample space or exhaustive search. Section 4 identifies an easier (r=2) regime after semicomplete reduction, culminating in a kernel with at most (3k-1) variables. Those results rule out presenting the prior-triage planted proposal as an honest Track-A theorem claim.
+An efficient distribution-specific algorithm definitely exists: reverse the paper’s Section 4 clause expansion and run exact Gaussian elimination over GF(2). Its expected cost is (O(m+n^3)). On eight shipping instances it solved 8/8, averaging **411,734 counted scalar operations and 0.003601 seconds** (maximum 457,731 operations and 0.004558 seconds). This successful algorithm is disclosed as reference_algorithm, not misreported as a failed attack.
 
-This is Track B. The distribution-specific reference algorithm groups the four-clause parity blocks and applies exact Gaussian elimination over GF(2), with complexity (O(m\log m+n^3)). On eight hard instances it solved 8/8, averaging **26,145 scalar operations and 0.008962 seconds** (maximum 27,419 operations and 0.019651 seconds). The compact route is to recognize (A^2=I) and apply the recovered operator once more: **160 binary XORs** at the shipping preset. The reference route is easy for a machine but not realistically executable unaided in the prompt; the short route depends on seeing the involution.
+The compact route recognizes the repeated-pair core and the invariant (A^2=I), then evaluates (x=Ab) with **160 XORs**. It still requires 3,840 literal inspections to recover the blocks. The benchmark tests whether a no-tool solver sees this compression rather than whether the family defeats algorithms.
 
