   513	\subsection{The Main Result for General  $r$}
   514	\begin{theorem}
   515	  \label{t95}
   516	  The problem $\MST$ is fixed-parameter tractable and can be solved in
   517	  time $O(m) + 2^{O(k^2)}$. Moreover, there exist
   518	(i) a polynomial-size bikernel from {\MST} to {\MLT},
   519	  and (ii) a polynomial-size kernel of {\MST}. In fact, there are
   520	such a bikernel and a kernel of size $O(k^2)$.
   521	\end{theorem}
   522	\vspace{-5pt}
   523	\begin{proof}
   524	  By Lemma~\ref{lem0} our problem is equivalent to that of deciding
   525	  whether or not there is a truth assignment to the variables $x_1, x_2,
   526	  \ldots, x_n$, so that
   527	\begin{equation}
   528	\label{e1}
   529	X(x_1, \ldots ,x_n) \geq k.
   530	\end{equation}
   531	Note that in particular this implies that if
   532	  $X$ is the zero polynomial, then any truth assignment satisfies
   533	  exactly a $(1-2^{-r})$ fraction of the original clauses.  By
   534	  Corollary~\ref{c93} and Lemma~\ref{l94}, $\Prob(X \geq
   535	  \frac{\sqrt{\Exp(X^2)}}{2\sqrt{b}})>0,$ where $b=9^r$ and
   536	  $\Exp(X^2)=\sum_{I\in {\cal S}}c^2_I \geq |{\cal S}|$; the last
   537	  inequality follows from the fact that each $|c_I|$ is a positive
   538	  integer. Therefore $\Prob(X \geq \frac{\sqrt{ |{\cal S}|}}{2\cdot
   539	    3^r})>0$. Now, if $k\le \frac{\sqrt{|{\cal S}|}}{2\cdot 3^r}$ then
   540	there are $x_i \in \{-1,1\}$ such that
   541	  (\ref{e1}) holds,
   542	and there is an assignment for which the answer to $\MST$
   543	  is {\sc Yes}. Otherwise, $|{\cal S}| = O(k^2)$, and in fact even
   544	$\sum_{I\in {\cal S}}|c_I| \leq \sum_{I\in {\cal S}}c^2_I =O(k^2)$,
   545	that is, the total number of terms of the simplified polynomial,
   546	even when counted with multiplicities, is at most $O(k^2)$.
   547	
   548	  For any fixed $r$, the representation of a problem instance of $m$
   549	  clauses as a polynomial, and the simplification of this polynomial,
   550	  can be performed in time $O(m)$. If the number of nonzero terms of
   551	  this polynomial is larger than $4 \cdot 3^{2r} k^2$, then the answer
   552	  to the problem is {\sc Yes}. Otherwise, the polynomial has at most
   553	  $O(k^2)$ terms and depends on at most $O(k^2)$ variables, and its
   554	  maximum can be found in time $2^{O(k^2)}$.
   555	
   556	This completes the proof of the first part of the theorem.
   557	We next establish the second part. Given the simplified  polynomial
   558	$X$ as above, define a problem in {\MLT} with the variables
   559	$z_1, z_2, \ldots ,z_n$ as follows. For each
   560	nonzero  term $c_I \prod_{i \in I} x_i$  consider the linear equation
   561	$\sum_{i \in I} z_i =b$, where $b=0$ if $c_I$ is positive, and $b=1$
   562	if $c_I$ is negative, and either associate this equation with the
   563	weight $w_I=|c_I|$, or duplicate it $|c_I|$ times.  It is easy to check
   564	that this system of equations has an assignment $z_i$ satisfying at least
   565	$[\sum_{I \in {\cal S}} w_I+k]/2$ of the equations if and only if
   566	there are $x_i \in \{-1,1\}$ so that $X(x_1,x_2, \ldots ,x_n)
   567	\geq k$. This is shown by the transformation $x_i=(-1)^{z_i}$. See
   568	also \cite{HastadVenkatesh02} and \cite{GutinKimSzeiderYeo09a} for
   569	a similar discussion. Since, as explained above,  we may assume
   570	that $\sum_{I \in {\cal S}} |c_I| =O(k^2)$ (as otherwise  we know
   571	that the answer to our problem is  {\sc Yes}), this provides the
   572	required bikernel of size $O(k^2)$ to {\MLT}.
   573	
   574	It remains to prove the existence of a polynomial size kernel for
   575	the original problem. One way to do that is to apply
   576	Lemma \ref{lem:pk}. Indeed, {\MLT} is in NP, and
   577	{\MST} is NP-complete,
   578	implying the desired  result.
   579	
   580	It is also possible to give a
   581	direct proof, which shows that the problem admits a kernel
   582	of size at most $O(k^2)$. To do so, we replace each linear equation
   583	of at most $r$ variables
   584	by a set of $2^{r-1}$ clauses, so that if the variables $z_i$
   585	satisfy the equation, the corresponding Boolean variables $x_i=(-1)^{z_i}$  satisfy all
   586	these clauses, and if the variables $z_i$ do not satisfy the
   587	equation, then the variables $x_i$ above   satisfy only
   588	$2^{r-1}-1$ of the clauses. This is done as follows.
   589	
   590	Consider, first, a linear equation with exactly $r$ variables.
   591	After
   592	renumbering the variables, if needed,  a typical equation
   593	is of the form  $z_1+z_2 + \cdots +z_r=b$, where the sum is over
   594	$\mathbb{F}_2$ and $b \in \{0,1\}$. There are exactly $2^{r-1}$
   595	Boolean assignments $\delta=(\delta_1,\delta_2, \ldots , \delta_r)$
   596	for the variables $z_i$
   597	that do {\bf not} satisfy  the equation. For each such assignment
   598	$\delta$ let $C_{\delta}$ be the clause consisting of $r$ literals,
   599	where the literal number $i$ is $x_i$ if $\delta_i=1$ and is
   600	$\overline{x_i}$ if $\delta_i=0$. Note that if the variables
   601	$z_1,z_2, \ldots ,z_r$ satisfy the  above equation, then
   602	$(z_1,z_2,\ldots ,z_r)$ is not one of the vectors $\delta$
   603	considered, and hence each of the clauses $C_{\delta}$
   604	constructed contains at
   605	least one satisfied literal when $x_i=(-1)^{z_i}$. Therefore, in this
   606	case all clauses are
   607	satisfied. A similar argument shows that if the variables $z_i$ do
   608	not satisfy the equation, there will  be exactly one non-satisfied
   609	clause, namely the one corresponding to the vector
   610	$\delta=(z_1,z_2, \ldots ,z_r)$. 
   611	
   612	The construction can be extended
   613	to equations with less than $r$ variables. Indeed,
   614	the only property
   615	used in the transformation above is that there are exactly
   616	$2^{r-1}$ Boolean assignments  for the variables  $z_1,z_2, \ldots
   617	,z_r$ that do not satisfy the equation. If the equation
   618	has only $(1 \leq)~ s<r$ variables, add to these variables an arbitrary set
   619	of $r-s$ of the other variables, and consider the set of all
   620	Boolean assignments to this augmented set of variables that do not
   621	satisfy the equation. Here, too, there are exactly $2^{r-1}$ such
   622	assignments  and we can thus repeat the construction above in this
   623	case as well.
   624	
   625	The above procedure transforms a set of $W$ linear equations over
   626	$\mathbb{F}_2$
   627	into a multiset of $2^{r-1} W$ clauses. Moreover, if some truth
   628	assignment  does not satisfy exactly $\ell$ equations, then the same
   629	assignment does not satisfy the same number, $\ell$, of clauses.
   630	In particular, there is an assignment satisfying all equations
   631	but $(W-k)/2$ of them if and only if  there is an assignment
   632	satisfying all clauses but $(W-k)/2$ of them. This means that
   633	among
   634	the $m=2^{r-1}W$ clauses, the number of satisfied ones is
   635	$m-(W-k)/2=[(2^r-1)m+2^{r-1}k]/2^r$. This reduces an instance of
   636	{\MLT} with $W$ equations and parameter $k$ to an instance of {\MST}
   637	with $2^{r-1}W$ clauses and parameter $2^{r-1}k$. Since $r$ is a
   638	constant, this provides the required kernel of size $O(k^2)$,
   639	completing the proof.
   640	\iffalse
