-## What the problem is
+## What the family is
 
-[Alon et al., *Solving MAX-r-SAT Above a Tight Lower Bound*](https://arxiv.org/abs/0907.4573) defines an exact (r)-CNF as a multiset of clauses containing exactly (r) distinct literals. For (r=3), (m) clauses, and scaled surplus (k), the task is to satisfy at least ((7m+k)/8) clauses. This family sets (k=m), so the witness must satisfy every displayed clause.
+[Alon et al., *Solving MAX-r-SAT Above a Tight Lower Bound*](https://arxiv.org/abs/0907.4573) defines an exact (r)-CNF as a multiset of clauses, each containing exactly (r) distinct literals. For (r=3), (m) clauses, and the paper's scaled surplus (k), the question is whether some truth assignment satisfies at least ((7m+k)/8) clauses. This family sets (k=m), so a witness must satisfy every clause.
 
-The solver receives the signed 3-CNF itself and returns one bit per variable. The verifier checks shape, evaluates every literal, counts satisfied clauses exactly, and compares with the integer target. It never reads the planted answer.
+The solver receives the native signed 3-CNF. For each group of four clauses it also receives the exact GF(2) parity equation that the group enforces; this is redundant data obtained by the paper's own Section 3 transformation, not an external surrogate. The answer is one bit per variable. `verify` checks its shape, evaluates the clauses exactly, and compares the satisfied count with the target without reading `inst["answer"]`.
 
-The certificate is known by inverse generation. A random assignment is sampled first and evaluated under an invertible binary operator (A=I+N), where (N^2=0), hence (A^2=I). Each resulting three-variable parity equation is converted to the four ordinary clauses specified in the paper. Same-format dependent parity rows add crowding without changing the unique solution.
+Generation is inverse. A uniformly random assignment (x) is sampled first. For (n=2h), the core uses a hidden cycle (p) and (A=I+N), where both rows belonging to pair (i) have the same (N)-part (x_{p(i)}\mathbin{\mathrm{xor}}x_{h+p(i)}). Hence (N^2=0) and (A^2=I). The generator computes (b=Ax), randomly renames variables, adds same-format consistent decoy rows on unused pairs, and expands every parity row into the four clauses from the proof of Theorem 1. The core is invertible, so the satisfying assignment is unique.
 
-## Why it is Track B
+## Why this is Track B
 
-This is not a Track-A distributional-hardness claim. Theorem 1 gives a general (O(m)+2^{O(k^2)}) algorithm and an (O(k^2)) kernel; the paragraph after its proof explains witness recovery by a bounded-independence sample space or exhaustive search. Thus small (k) is an easy regime and was deliberately avoided. Section 5 gives an even sharper (3k-1)-variable kernel for reduced Max-2-SAT, so this family uses (r=3).
+This is not a distributional Track-A claim. Theorem 1 gives a general (O(m)+2^{O(k^2)}) algorithm and an (O(k^2))-size kernel for fixed (r). The paragraph immediately after its proof explains how to recover a witness using a bounded-independence sample space in the large-polynomial case and exhaustive search in the kernel case. Section 4 gives the still stronger (3k-1)-variable kernel for reduced Max-2-SAT. Those are the easy regimes this family must disclose; it uses (r=3) and (k=m), which grows with the instance.
 
-An efficient distribution-specific algorithm definitely exists: reverse the paper’s Section 4 clause expansion and run exact Gaussian elimination over GF(2). Its expected cost is (O(m+n^3)). On eight shipping instances it solved 8/8, averaging **411,734 counted scalar operations and 0.003601 seconds** (maximum 457,731 operations and 0.004558 seconds). This successful algorithm is disclosed as reference_algorithm, not misreported as a failed attack.
+An efficient distribution-specific algorithm also exists. Read the parity blocks and run exact Gaussian elimination over GF(2): expected (O(m+n^3)). On eight hard instances it solved 8/8, averaging **214,003 counted bit/scalar operations and 0.034645 seconds** (maximum 264,167 operations and 0.059125 seconds). It is reported as `reference_algorithm`, not disguised as a failed attack.
 
-The compact route recognizes the repeated-pair core and the invariant (A^2=I), then evaluates (x=Ab) with **160 XORs**. It still requires 3,840 literal inspections to recover the blocks. The benchmark tests whether a no-tool solver sees this compression rather than whether the family defeats algorithms.
+The compact route is to recognize the repeated two-variable supports, reconstruct the paired core operator, notice (A^2=I), and evaluate (x=Ab). The rendered parity summaries remove any hidden clause-decoding arithmetic; applying the operator takes **288 XORs** at the hard preset. Finding the row pairing still requires scanning 176 summaries, a limitation recorded below.
 
