 ## Caveats
 
-The family becomes easy for any XOR-aware solver; Gaussian elimination is the explicit reference. Setting (k=m) is the full-satisfiability extreme, not the near-lower-bound regime that motivates much of the paper. The 0/200,000 measurement concerns a uniform prior over syntactically valid bit vectors; it says nothing about CDCL, WalkSAT, learned heuristics, or algebraic attacks. No external modern SAT solver was run, though the stronger structure-specific exact solver was. The unsigned stable-color canonical key is an invariant rather than a complete hypergraph isomorphism test, so rare collisions remain possible despite 20/20 distinctness. Finally, the required four-vendor evidence is blocked by external quota and this result must not be called oracle-hardened until those runs complete.
+Any XOR-aware implementation solves the family efficiently; Gaussian elimination is the explicit reference algorithm. The instance is at the full-satisfiability extreme $k=m$, not close to the tight lower bound that motivates the paper. The 0/200,000 guess result is only for the uniform prior on syntactically valid bit vectors and says nothing about CDCL, learned SAT heuristics, Gröbner-basis methods, or other algebraic attacks. No external SAT package was run, although the more structure-specific exact Gaussian solver and a substantial parity-aware randomized repair were run.
+
+The 288-operation figure counts exact XORs after the involution and row pairing are recognized; scanning 176 summaries to discover repeated pairs is additional comparison work. The canonical key is a stable color-refinement invariant, not a complete hypergraph-isomorphism test, so rare collisions remain possible. Most importantly, the current oracle-pool evidence is missing because of external quota: `SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY="hard"` names the candidate preset, but this result is not ready to submit as hardened until `harden.py` produces a real verdict.
