   121	}
   122	
   123	NOTES = r"""
   124	Definition and easy regimes. Section 2 defines an r-CNF formula as a multiset
   125	of clauses, each containing exactly r distinct literals and no complementary
   126	pair. It defines Max-r-Sat above the tight lower bound by the target
   127	((2^r-1)m+k)/2^r. Lemma 2 identifies the exact polynomial identity relating
   128	that target to a degree-r multilinear polynomial. Theorem 1 is the decisive
   129	easy-regime result: for fixed r the decision and witness problem takes
   130	O(m)+2^{O(k^2)}, with an O(k^2) kernel. Consequently this family does not claim
   131	Track-A hardness and does not keep k small; with r=3 and k=m its target is m,
   132	and k grows linearly with the number of core and decoy parity rows.
   133	
   134	Paper construction. In the direct-kernel part of the proof of Theorem 1,
   135	Section 4.3 replaces every equation z_i1+...+z_ir=b over GF(2) with the 2^(r-1)
   136	clauses falsified by assignments that violate the equation. Satisfying an
   137	equation satisfies every clause in its block, while violating it falsifies
   138	exactly one. This module uses precisely that native 3-CNF construction with
   139	r=3; it is not a graph or finite-field surrogate shown to the solver.
   140	
   141	Generation certificate. For n=2h, choose a directed h-cycle p and define A by
   142	  (Ax)_i     = x_i xor x_p(i) xor x_(h+p(i)),
   143	  (Ax)_(h+i) = x_(h+i) xor x_p(i) xor x_(h+p(i)).
   144	Writing A=I+N, both rows belonging to a pair have the same N-part. Therefore
   145	N^2=0 and A^2=I over GF(2), so A is invertible. The generator samples x first,
   146	computes b=Ax, randomly renames variables, and expands each row. Extra rows
   147	are sampled on unused variable pairs, evaluated at the same x, and expanded
   148	identically. The held witness is x carried through the renaming. No SAT,
   149	Max-SAT, kernel, or linear solver is invoked by make_instance.
   150	
   151	Step-0 discrimination and Track B. The reverse of the paper's construction is
   152	an efficient algorithm: group four clauses having the same unsigned triple,
   153	read their parity, and apply GF(2) Gaussian elimination. It is reported as the
   154	successful reference_algorithm, never disguised as a failed attack. The
   155	compact route instead recognizes the repeated-pair cycle and A^2=I, evaluating
   156	x=Ab with two XORs per output bit. The exact mechanical and compact costs at
   157	the shipping preset are recorded by selftest.
   158	
   159	Output contract. The renderer gives the GF(2) equation enforced by each
   160	four-clause block as a redundant exact summary. This is the same transformation
   161	proved in Section 4.3, keeps all native clauses visible, and makes the intended
   162	post-insight cost exactly the reported two XORs per answer bit rather than
   163	quietly charging the solver for reconstructing every parity right-hand side.
   164	
   165	Planting hygiene and attacks. Every parity row, core or decoy, is rendered by
   166	the same four-clause truth table; within every block each variable occurs
   167	positively twice and negatively twice. Variables are independently relabelled,
   168	and row units and literals are shuffled. Literal majority, occurrence outliers,
   169	public-order periodicity, bounded greedy repair, randomized parity repair, and an uncoupled parity-
   170	RHS vote are all tested over eight shipping seeds. The pair co-degree signal
   171	is deliberately present: it is the structural clue needed for the short route,
   172	not a correlation with the sampled answer bits.
   173	""".strip()
   174	
